<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>4.4.6: Lesson Synthesis</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00897</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.4.6: Lesson Synthesis</md:title>
    <md:uuid>15cc8341-0d14-4e19-b3ba-5e5d10bafb80</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>Display for all to see the equations \(f(x)=5x+3\) and \(g(x)=10x-4\). Ask students:</para>
<list>
  <item>“How would you describe to a classmate who is absent today what each equation means? What would you say to help them make sense of these?” (Each equation gives the rule of a function. The rule for \(f\) says that, to get the output, we multiply the input by 5 and add 3. The rule for \(g\) says that the output is 10 times the input, minus 4.)</item>
  <item>“How do the rules help us find the value of \(f(10)\) or \(g(10)\)?” (If we substitute 10 for \(x\) in each equation and evaluate the expression, we would have the values of \(f\) or \(g\) at \(x=10\), which are 53 and 96, respectively.)</item>
  <item>“Is it possible to graph a function described this way? How?” (We could create a table of values and find the coordinate pairs at different \(x\)-values. Or, if a rule is expressed as a linear equation, we could use it to identify the slope and vertical intercept of the graph.)</item>
</list></section>
</content>
